        {
          "text": "LEETCODE 56 · MEDIUM",
          "at": [
            800,
            262
          ],
          "accent": true
        }
      ],
      "problem": {
        "statement": "Given an array of *intervals* where intervals[i] = [start_i, end_i], merge all overlapping intervals, and return an array of the non-overlapping intervals that cover all the intervals in the input.",
        "example": [
          "Input:  intervals = [[1,3], [2,6], [8,10], [15,18]]",
          "Output: [[1,6], [8,10], [15,18]]",
          "[1,3] and [2,6] overlap, so they merge into [1,6]"
        ],
        "constraints": [
          "1 <= intervals.length <= 10^4",
          "intervals[i].length == 2",
          "0 <= start_i <= end_i <= 10^4"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "eyebrow": "In plain English",
      "chapter": "Problem",
      "panel": false,
      "dur": 9,
      "caption": "What the interviewer is really asking.",
      "plain": {
        "asking": "Some ranges overlap. *Glue the overlapping ones together* and return what is left.",
        "points": [
          "*You get:* ranges like [1, 3] and [2, 6].",
          "*You return:* the glued ranges, like [1, 6].",
          "*The catch:* the ranges can arrive in any order."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "eyebrow": "The picture",
      "dur": 8,
      "caption": "Each range is a bar on a number line. Here they arrive *out of order*, so overlaps are hard to spot.",
      "form": {
        "type": "shapes",
        "items": [
          {
            "shape": "intervals",
